## 1. Introduction
Since 2009, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of organic– inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has rapidly increased from the initial $3.8\%$ to $25.5\%$ [1–3]. Comparing to the organic– inorganic hybrid PSCs, which are easily affected by thermal stress and humid environment, resulting in poor device stability [2–5], the all-inorganic PSCs have aroused increasing interest of researchers, since the substitution of organic cations by $C s^{+}$ ions can effectively improve the stability of PSCs [6–8]. Recently, Liang et al. prepared all-inorganic PSCs based on $\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}$ by defect engineering, they demonstrate excellent stability in air for more than two months [9]. Wang et al. modified the surface of $\mathsf{C s P b I}_{2}\mathsf{B r}$ with ${\sf C}s_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P t I}_{6}$ , which can delay the degradation of perovskite and enhance the moisture resistance of PSCs [10]. Yu et al. reported that bilateral interface modification of $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ by $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ nanocrystals and this approach improves the devices stability [11].
However, $\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}$ and $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{2}\mathsf{B r}$ are not stable and quickly degrades to a yellow non-perovskite phase at room temperature, which basically does not absorb much sun light [12–14]. Although, $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ has excellent stability, but the wide band gap $(2.3\mathrm{eV})$ limits the absorption below $530~\mathrm{nm}$ , thus hindering the final performance of the corresponding PSCs [15]. In contrast, $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ which has excellent phase stability and a suitable $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}},$ has become a more perfect item. Recently, we prepared a $19\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick layer of P3HT: PCBM heterojunction on the surface of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film and achieved a champion PCE $(11.54\%)$ of carbon-based allinorganic $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs [16].
As a conventional electron transport material, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs) can be used to fabricate planar or mesoporous ETLs for PSCs [17–18]. However, the planar $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs ETLs generally have the problems of hysteresis, interfacial recombination and low electron mobility, so the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs ETLs are more widely used in PSCs [19–20]. Nevertheless, mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs ETLs are usually stacked by tiny nanoparticles which are detrimental to the rapid transmission of electrons [21–22]. In addition, the large number of defect traps in the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs ETLs lead to an increase in the charge recombination rate, thus reducing photovoltaic performance [23].
Three-dimensional (3D) nanoarrays have the characteristics of inhibiting charge recombination and improving electron transport [24–27]. Earlier, one-dimensional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanorods arrays were investigated as ETLs in organic–inorganic hybrid PSCs [28–31]. Park et al. reported length controllable rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanorods PSCs with a best efficiency of $9.4\%$ [29]. Liu et al. adjusted the energy band structure of the PSCs by doping Ni in rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs [32]. Shankar et al. detailed the effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanorods morphology on the device performance, achieving a champion efficiency of $17.6\%$ [33–35]. Since the crystal structure of the rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is more stable, it is easier to be synthesized directly on conductive substrates by solution processes [36–37]. Nevertheless, the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is reported to have higher electron mobility and charge diffusion coefficient than the rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [38–39]. Therefore, anatase 3D $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoarrays ETLs based PSCs are expected to have better photovoltaic performance than that of the rutile counterparts. However, as far as we know, the previously reported $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanorods arrays are mostly rutile phase, there are a few reports about the anatase 3D $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoarrays as the ETLs for PSCs.
Here, we have successfully synthesized single-crystal 3D anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoarrays ETLs $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs) directly on the fluorine doped conductive glasses (FTO) by hydrothermal method. By adjusting the concentrations of $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ in the reaction solutions, the morphology of the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs can be adjusted from cluster to sheet. Compared to the conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NCs ETLs $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs), the 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs have better light transmittance and charge transport properties. Moreover, they can assist formation of high quality $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ with larger crystal grains. The $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs achieve high PCE values exceeding $10\%$ , which is obviously higher than the pristine one $(7.16\%)$ . Additionally, it is encouraging that the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs can shield ultraviolet (UV) light to obtain much higher UV stable $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ and the corresponding devices than the ones with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs. This work presents an effective method for preparing high-efficiency and UV stable carbon-based all-inorganic PSCs.
## 2. Experiment section
2.1. Synthesis of $T i O_{2}$ nanoparticles and 3D anatase $T i O_{2}$ nanoarrays
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles and $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs were synthesized with the same method as previously reported [40–41]. Figure S1 illustrates the main experiment procedures for preparing $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs and 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs.
## 2.2. Fabrication of carbon-based $C s P b I B r_{2}$ PSCs
The laser-patterned FTO glasses with size of $1.5\times1.5\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ were consecutively cleaned by detergent and sonicated in isopropanol, acetone, deionized water and ethanol and finally treated with UV-ozone for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs were prepared by spin-coating the ethanol solution of pre-synthesized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $\mathrm{'}5\sim6\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}}$ ) on FTOs at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s and heated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{\min}$ , soon afterward spin-coated once again and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs were directly synthesized on the FTOs by hydrothermal method with $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ solution. For the fabrication of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film, the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ precursor $(1.0~\mathrm{M})$ was prepared by full dissolution of CsBr $(212.8~\mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ , $\mathsf{P b I}_{2}$ $(230.5~\mathrm{mg})$ and $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ ( $183.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{,}$ in DMSO ( $\mathbf{\rho}_{1}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L}$ . Firstly, the precursor was spin-coated on the substrates at 1000 rpm for $10s$ and then at $3000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and pre-crystallized at $32\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ for $30~s$ . Afterwards, the substrates were immediately placed on a hotplate at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ and then cooled down to room temperature naturally. Finally, the carbon paste was doctor-bladed on the top of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ layer, followed by drying at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 15 mins to
remove the solvent. Figure S2 illustrates the main experiment procedures for preparing carbon-based $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ all-inorganic PSCs with $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs.
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 shows the top-view scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs and $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs. As can be seen from Fig. 1a, the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL shows porous morphology. Fig. 1(b-d) show the morphologies of $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs grown on FTO using different concentration of $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ solutions. It can be seen that the FTO surfaces are completely covered by ${3\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs. As the concentration of $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ increased from $31\mathrm{mg}$ to $62~\mathrm{mg}$ and $124{\mathrm{mg}}$ , the morphologies of composed particles of the 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs change from square-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles $(31~\mathrm{mg})$ to square-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sheets $(62~\mathrm{mg})$ and enlarged square-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sheets $(124{\mathrm{~mg}})$ .
Fig. 2a shows the $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\ \mathrm{mg}\right)$ ) ETL. Compared with the standard card (JCPDS #21– 1272), the as prepared sample is classified as anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Furthermore, the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL is characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), high resolution TEM and selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) as shown in Fig. 2b-e. One can see that the 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL shows cubic shape from the TEM image (Fig. 2b) and the interplanar space is about $0.3343\pm0.0063\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 2c and 2d). The SAED pattern (Fig. 2e) of $3\mathsf{D}\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ ETL indicates the single crystal feature and the diffraction spots can be indexed to the (101), (220), (204), (404) and (400) crystal planes. In addition, the $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL displays a slightly higher root mean square roughness (RMS) of $31.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ from Figure S3b and d compared to that of the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(24.8~\mathrm{nm})$ (Figure S3a and c).
The ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) absorption spectra of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs are displayed in Figure S4. The $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs show better UV–vis absorption compared with the one on the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL, and the one prepared on the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL from $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ solution with a concentration of $62~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) shows the best performance. In Fig. 3a, the absorption intensity of the $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}/\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CsPbIBr}_{2}/3\mathrm{D}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ $62~\mathrm{mg}$ films have clear difference in the same light absorption range and the latter one has higher absorption intensities in the visible light absorption region. It can be confirmed that the two samples both show absorption peaks at $570\mathrm{nm}$ and absorption starting points at $590\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to an $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ of $2.10\ \mathrm{eV}$ .
Energy level data of 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL are calculated to evaluate the energy level matching among these functional layers. The Tauc plots as shown in Fig. 3b reveals that the $\mathtt{E_{g}}$ of $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL is 3.39 eV. From ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) data as shown in Fig. 3c, the Fermi level $\left(\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{F}}\right)$ of $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL is $-4.05\ \mathrm{\eV},$ , which is obtained from the equation of $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{F}}~{=}~\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{cut-off}}$ (cut-off binding energy, $17.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ as shown in Fig. 3c)-21.22 eV (emission energy from He irradiation). The valence band maximum $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{VB}})$ of 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL is $-7.37\mathrm{eV}$ from the equation of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbb{E}_{\mathtt{V B}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathtt{F}}-\mathbb{E}_{\mathtt{o n}}.}\end{array}$ set (onset binding energy, $3.32\mathrm{eV}$ as shown in Fig. 3c). The conduction band minimum $\left(\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}\right)$ of $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL is $-3.98$ eV being determined from $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ and $\tt{E}_{\tt V B}$ . Combining with corresponding energy level data of other functional layers from literature [42], the energy level alignments of PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ ETLs are drawn in Fig. 3d. We can see that the 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL can optimize energy level alignment of the device to achieve better photovoltaic performance.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra were measured to reveal the difference of charge transfer dynamics of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ and $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs, respectively. The PL spectra of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL and $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs from different concentrations of $\mathrm{TiF_{4}}$ are shown in Figure S5. Fig. 4a reveals that the PL intensity of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ $62{\mathrm{~mg}}^{\cdot}$ ) ETL is significantly lower than the one on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL, indicating that the ${3\mathsf{D}}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\mathrm{mg},$ ETL can be more conducive to charge extraction than the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL. Meanwhile, the PL peak of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL has an obvious blue shift compared to the one on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL, indicating an obviously reduced defect state [43]. Fig. 4b shows the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}^{\cdot}$ ) ETLs. The change trends are consistent with the PL results. The data are fitted by the exponential decay function: $\mathrm{I(t)}=\mathrm{A_{1}}\mathrm{exp(-t/\tau_{1})}+\mathrm{A_{2}}\mathrm{exp(-t/\tau_{2})}+\mathrm{B}$ $\mathbf{\dot{A}}_{1}$ and $\mathsf{A}_{2}$ represent the attenuation amplitude, B represents the constant of baseline offset, $\tau_{1}$ represents the rapid attenuation of trap-mediated nonradiative recombination, and $\tau_{2}$ represents the slow attenuation related to radiation recombination). The average PL decay time $(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}})$ can be calculated by the following formula [44]: $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}~=$ $(\mathsf{A}_{1}\uptau_{1}^{2}+\mathsf{A}_{2}\uptau_{2}^{2})/(\mathsf{A}_{1}\uptau_{1}+\mathsf{A}_{2}\uptau_{2}).$ . According to the results (Table S2), the $3{\mathrm{D}}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , 62 mg) ETL can accelerate electron extraction due to the much lower $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ $(2.36~\mathrm{ns})$ than that of the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL (9.09 ns). Fig. 4c shows the Tafel curves of the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\ \mathrm{mg},$ ETLs. Compared with the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL, the 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETL exhibits higher conductivity, which also indicates that it can be more favorable for charge transport.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted to investigate the charge transport and recombination at interfaces. Fig. 4d displays Nyquist plots of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with C$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}^{\cdot}$ ) ETLs under AM 1.5 G light illumination. The equivalent circuit model shown in Fig. 4d includes charge transfer resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ and series resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{s}\right)$ . Compared with the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSC with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL, the smaller $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ value of the one with 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL $\mathrm{(TiF_{4},62~m g)}$ indicates that the $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $\mathrm{TiF_{4}}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}.$ shows more efficient electron extraction at the ET $\mathsf{L}/\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ interface, thereby improving short-circuit current density $(J s c)$ and fill factor (FF). Fig. 4e shows the Mott-Schottky $(\mathsf{M}-\mathsf{S})$ curves of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ ,
$62\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETLs. Comparing to the built-in potential $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{bi}})$ of the sample with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL (1.604 V), the higher $\mathsf{V}_{\mathsf{b i}}$ of the one with $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ 0 $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ETL (1.725 V) means more effective separation of photo-generated electron-hole pairs in the device [45–46].
To further understand the carrier transfer dynamics in the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs, light intensity dependent open-circuit voltage $(V_{O C})$ , transient photocurrent (TPC) and transient photovoltage (TPV) are measured as shown in Fig. 4f-h. The $V_{O C}$ versus logscaled I plots are linearly fitted by the following formula [47]: $\mathsf{V}_{0\mathsf{C}}=(\mathsf{n k T}\mathsf{l n I})$ $\textit{\textbf{/q+C}}$ (n represents an ideal factor, K represents the Boltzmann constant, T represents absolute temperature, I represents the incident light intensity, q represents the elementary charge and C represents a constant). The n values of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(TiF_{4},62~m g)}$ ) ETLs are 2.001 and 1.579, respectively. The latter has smaller n value, demonstrating that the $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ ETL is able to limit recombination more efficiently.
The TPC can reflect the extraction and transport properties of carriers, while the TPV can monitor the recombination of charge carriers in the device. It can be seen from Fig. $4\mathrm{g}$ that for the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETLs, the photocurrent decay constants are $10.00~\upmu s$ and $4.46~\upmu s$ , respectively. It is obvious that the latter has a much faster photocurrent decay rate, indicating that the carrier extraction and transportation are more efficient, which may be owing to the higher crystallinity of the 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL. Meanwhile, as shown in Fig. 4h, the photovoltage decay lifetime of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSC with $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETL $(0.513~\mathrm{ms})$ is notably slower than that of the one with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(0.046~\mathrm{ms}^{\cdot}$ ), which shows that the recombination of carriers is greatly suppressed in the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSC with 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL.
Based on the space-charge-limited-current (SCLC) method, the trap density $(n_{t r a p})$ of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETLs are analyzed. Fig. 4i shows the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves measured under dark condition. Both devices exhibit ohmic characteristics at low voltages, and the current density increases non-linearly when the voltage exceeds the trap fill limit $(V_{T F L})$ . $V_{T F L}$ is related to the $n_{t r a p}$ according to the following formula [48]: $\mathrm{V_{TFL}}{=}(\mathrm{qn}_{\mathrm{trap}}\mathrm{L}^{2})/(2\upvarepsilon\upvarepsilon_{0})$ (q represents the basic charge of the electron, L represents the thickness of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film, e represents the vacuum dielectric constant, $\scriptstyle\varepsilon_{0}$ represents the relative dielectric constant). The $V_{T F L}$ of the devices with $C-$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETLs are $1.519\mathrm{~V~}$ and $1.372\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively. Therefore, the 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL can reduce defects in the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film, which is favorable for improving photovoltaic performance of the device.
The effect of the concentration of $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ in the reaction solutions on the photovoltaic performance of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs was evaluated. Figure S6 and Table S3 show the $J{-}V$ curves and photovoltaic parameters of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs prepared with different concentrations of $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ . From the data, $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs are beneficial to improving the photovoltaic performance of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs. When the concentration of $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ is $62~\mathrm{mg}$ , the PCE of the devices can reach the best value. Fig. 5a summarizes the $J{-}V$ curves of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\ \mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ ) ETLs. The enhancement of photovoltaic performance is obvious by substituting $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL with $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},$ $62\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL, and the one with 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{\bar{IiO}}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETL can achieve a much higher PCE of $10.65\%$ than that of the one with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(7.16\%)$ . In addition, the incident photocurrent conversion efficiency (IPCE) data (Fig. 5c) also prove that the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSC with $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL exhibits higher integrated current density $(10.51~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) than that of the one with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(9.76~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ). Fig. 5b shows that the steady-state $J_{S C}$ and PCE of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSC with $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ ) ETL (after 10 s measurement under light) are $10.48~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $9.63\%$ , respectively, which are obviously higher than those of the one with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $\cdot8.42\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ and $7.02\%$ . Normally, the ion migration and trap defects in the interface may generate hysteresis behavior [49,50]. Figure S7 shows $\mathsf{J}^{-\mathsf{V}}$ curves of the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}.$ and C$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ PSCs with reverse and forward scans. Corresponding photovoltaic parameters are shown in Table S4. The hysteresis index (HI) of 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}.$ ) and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ PSCs is 0.089 and 0.145, respectively, so the hysteresis is effectively suppressed in the 3D$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) PSCs.
For comparison, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs synthesized with $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ (marked as ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{4}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ ETLs and $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs, respectively) by the same amount $\left(62{\mathrm{~mg}}\right)$ were prepared. Figure S8 shows surface morphologies of these $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs. The difference can be easily observed, that is the FTO surface is covered by numbers of nanodot and nanowire -like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles after hydrothermal treatment with ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{4}$ nevertheless, distinguishing feature of well crystalline square-like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles are formed on the same substrate after hydrothermal treatment with $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ . Figure S9 and Table S5 show photovoltaic performance of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{4}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs. The difference also can be obviously found, that is the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs show much better photovoltaic performance than those of the ones with ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{4}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ ETLs. In details, the average and champion $V_{O C},J_{S C},$ FF, and PCE values of the devices with $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs are all higher than those of the latter ones, and the highest PCE of the former ones is $10.65\ \%$ , whereas that of the latter ones is $8.38\%$ .
Furthermore, the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with commonly used 2, 20, 7, 70- tetrakis (N, N-p- dimethoxy-phenylamino)-9, ${\mathfrak{g}}^{}$ -spirobifluorene (Spiro) hole transport material and Au electrodes $\left(\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\right.$ $\mathrm{CsPbIBr_{2}/S p i r o/A u)}$ are fabricated for comparison. From the corresponding data shown in Figure S10 and Table S6, it is found that the carbon-electrode devices can achieve similar photovoltaic performance as that of the Spiro/Au-based ones and the difference is mainly originated from the lower FF values of the carbonelectrode devices. Considering the easier fabricating processes and much lower production cost, carbon-electrode devices still have obviously advantages.
Fig. 6a and Figure S11 show the transmittance spectra of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs, from which one can find that the 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs not only have good transparency as $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL but also show much lower transmittance in the UV range, namely, they have UV light shielding function, which is in favor of overcoming the big drawback of UV induced instability of conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based ETLs. In order to demonstrate the improved UV stability, the normalized PCEs of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}.$ ETLs were measured after continuous UV illumination $(60\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}.$ ) for 7 days, as shown in Fig. 6b. The $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETLs manifest a dramatic decay in PCE to $83.5\%$ of their initial value under UV aging for 7 days, while the PCE values of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETLs decay at a tardier rate and retain nearly $94.9\%$ of their initial value under the same condition.
The color changes of the film in the photos shown in Fig. 6c can also verify the better UV stability of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ETL. It is seen that the color of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL after UV aging is not so homogeneous as that of the fresh one, whereas the UV aged $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL shows nearly the same color as the fresh one.
Fig. 6d and 6e show the top-view SEM images of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3\mathsf{D}\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ETLs, respectively. It is clearly observed that the $3{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{-T i O}}_{2}$ (TiF4, $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETL can well assist the formation of larger $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ crystal grains compared with the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL. The corresponding XRD patterns shown in Fig. 3f also confirm the synthesis of higher crystallinity of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film assisted by the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL. Since the nucleation of perovskite on the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL is heterogeneous nucleation, the free energy required for nucleation can be expressed by the following formula: DGheterogeneous $=\Delta G$ homogeneous$\times{\bf\delta f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}.$ $\Theta),\operatorname{f}(\Theta)=(2-3\mathrm{cos}\Theta+\mathrm{cos}^{3}\Theta)/$ 4, $\Theta\in\left[0,2\pi\right]$ [51].
Therefore, the wettability of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL surface plays an important role in the crystallization of perovskite. Figure S12 shows the contact angles of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ precursors on the surfaces of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ and $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETLs. It is obvious that the contact angle on the surface of ${3\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL is smaller $(17.05^{\circ})$ than that of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(34.15^{\circ})$ , which is helpful for crystal growth of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ on $3\mathsf{D}\mathsf{-T i O}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\ \mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL.
Additionally, the changes of micro morphology of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films shown in Fig. 6g and $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ reveal that the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL appears clear pinholes and traces of corrosion, nevertheless, the one on $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL nearly maintains the same morphology as the fresh one. Therefore, these data well demonstrate the higher UV stability of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film and the corresponding PSC that prepared with ${3\mathsf{D}}{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. The corresponding XRD patterns shown in Fig. 6i further confirm the better UV stability of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ) ETL. The main diffraction peaks at $15.29^{\circ}$ , $21.59^{\circ}$ and $30.48^{\circ}$ of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ films are corresponding to the (100), (110), and (200) planes. With continuous UV aging for 7 days, the XRD patterns of $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL shows a higher peak intensity of (100) crystal face, while the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ film prepared on 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ , $62~\mathrm{mg}$ ) ETL presents slight changes [52].
Finally, the unencapsulated $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},$ $62{\mathrm{~mg}}.$ ) ETLs were prepared for long-term stability tests [53–55]. As shown in Fig. 6j, one group was stored in an ambient atmosphere with relative humidity (R.H.) about $10\sim25\%$ , and the device maintains $89.4\%$ of the original PCE after $720\mathrm{~h~}$ ; the other one was kept at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{{^C}}$ on a hotplate in an $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ glovebox, which can retain about $94.5\%$ the original PCE after $720\mathrm{h}$ . Therefore, the PSCs with 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TiF}_{4},62\mathrm{mg},$ ) ETLs show good UV, humidity and thermal stability.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, single crystal anatase 3D- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs have been successfully grown on the FTO substrates directly by hydrothermal method with $\mathrm{TiF}_{4}$ solution. The energy level matching and charge transfer dynamics can be well optimized, which bring about obviously enhanced photovoltaic performance of the $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs with $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs compared with those devices with either C$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs or $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs prepared with $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ solution. The champion $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSC with $3\mathsf{D}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ ETL yields a PCE as high as $10.65\%$ , which is close to that of the device containing the commonly used Spiro hole transporter and Au electrode $(10.79\%)$ , and much higher than the one with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL prepared with $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ solution $(8.38\%)$ . Specially, the $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETL shows good UV light shielding function, which effectively overcomes the main drawback of UV instability of conventional $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs and is helpful to prepare high UV stable $\mathsf{C s P b I B r}_{2}$ PSCs. Furthermore, the devices with $3{\mathsf{D}}{-}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs also show good humidity stability and thermal stability. Therefore, the utilization of $3{\mathsf{D}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ ETLs may be a promising strategy for developing efficient and stable all-inorganic PSCs.
